WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.610 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:84%
The following
content is provided

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.370 align:middle line:84%
by MIT OpenCourseWare under
a Creative Commons license.

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:08.470 align:middle line:84%
Additional information
about our license

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.870 align:middle line:84%
and MIT OpenCourseWare
in general

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.730 align:middle line:90%
is available at ocw.mit.edu.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:18.640 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.530 align:middle line:90%
GEORGE CHURCH: OK.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:23.710 align:middle line:84%
Well, even though we've been
hinting at networks pretty

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.400 align:middle line:84%
heavily all the way
through the course,

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.910 align:middle line:84%
these are the three lectures
where we actually take it on.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:34.090 align:middle line:84%
We really started this at the
end of last week's lecture,

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.440 align:middle line:90%
so-called protein 2.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.830 align:middle line:84%
Where in the process of talking
about protein modifications

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.865 align:middle line:84%
and quantitating metabolites
and their interactions

00:00:41.865 --> 00:00:43.240 align:middle line:84%
with proteins, we
started talking

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:47.200 align:middle line:84%
about the sorts of sources of
data that you would have that

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:54.430 align:middle line:84%
would allow you to get at
a quantitative analysis

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:58.310 align:middle line:84%
of protein networks,
such as red blood cell.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:01.540 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
pick up on that theme

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:06.070 align:middle line:84%
by talking about macroscopic
continuous concentration

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.780 align:middle line:84%
gradients, and
then contrast that

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:11.020 align:middle line:84%
with mesoscopic or
discrete molecular numbers.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.410 align:middle line:84%
We're just going to very
briefly touch upon the issues

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:17.230 align:middle line:84%
in discriminating between
the stochastic modeling

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.960 align:middle line:90%
and the continuous modeling.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.915 align:middle line:84%
And it's a very
interesting connection

00:01:23.915 --> 00:01:25.540 align:middle line:84%
between the red blood
cell model, where

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:28.630 align:middle line:84%
you have a few examples
of cooperativity

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.670 align:middle line:84%
with modest Hill's
coefficients--

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.550 align:middle line:84%
to take that to an extreme
case where you have actually

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.760 align:middle line:84%
bistability, where you
have two stable modes which

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.750 align:middle line:84%
are separated by a very
cooperative interaction.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.050 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll talk about
copy number controls,

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.810 align:middle line:84%
another opportunity
for doing modeling

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:49.930 align:middle line:84%
either by macroscopic
continuous modeling

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:53.950 align:middle line:90%
or this stochastic modeling.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.770 align:middle line:84%
And then, after the break,
we'll talk about flux balance

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:01.930 align:middle line:84%
optimization, which I think is
a really exciting and clever way

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.210 align:middle line:84%
of leveraging the little
bits of information

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:07.690 align:middle line:84%
that you have about
very complicated

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:11.120 align:middle line:84%
regulatory networks,
biochemical networks.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.980 align:middle line:84%
So I think we've kind
of just barely touched

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.650 align:middle line:90%
upon this before.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:21.880 align:middle line:84%
The particular networks that
are being studied, how did they

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:22.810 align:middle line:90%
come to be studied?

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.450 align:middle line:90%
Why and how?

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.670 align:middle line:84%
And typically, what
they have in common

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:32.143 align:middle line:84%
is that they have large genetic
and biochemical kinetic data

00:02:32.143 --> 00:02:33.310 align:middle line:90%
sets to go with it-- and/or.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:36.010 align:middle line:90%


00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:39.100 align:middle line:84%
Right now there is no
model that describes

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:43.360 align:middle line:84%
all of the interesting
aspects of an entire cell

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.215 align:middle line:90%
or an entire organism.

00:02:45.215 --> 00:02:46.840 align:middle line:84%
Usually, there are
little pieces of it.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.180 align:middle line:84%
The closest to a whole
cell is the red blood cell.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:53.320 align:middle line:84%
And this is because there's
no biopolymer components

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.860 align:middle line:90%
to it, no biopolymer synthesis.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.450 align:middle line:84%
Today, we'll talk
a little bit more

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.190 align:middle line:84%
about these two
related topics, which

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:05.530 align:middle line:84%
is cell division cycle and
the segregation of chromosomes

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:07.570 align:middle line:90%
during the cell division.

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:15.040 align:middle line:84%
Here, the key point
is the critical nature

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.530 align:middle line:84%
of single molecules in
these that come into play

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.360 align:middle line:90%
in the dividing cells.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.540 align:middle line:84%
And in this, they'll be talking
about bistability, how there's

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:29.200 align:middle line:84%
a decision of either to take
the next step in dividing

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.520 align:middle line:90%
the cell or not.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:34.200 align:middle line:84%
And how that bistability can be
achieved either stochastically,

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.950 align:middle line:84%
where you're dealing
with the fluctuations

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.470 align:middle line:84%
of single molecules
greatly affecting a switch,

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:41.337 align:middle line:84%
such as a phase
lambda switch, or you

00:03:41.337 --> 00:03:43.420 align:middle line:84%
can have it involving a
large number of molecules,

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:47.440 align:middle line:84%
where stochastic seems
to play less of a role.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.350 align:middle line:84%
And then, at the end,
we'll talk about how

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.660 align:middle line:84%
we can do comparative
metabolism, where we literally

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:58.660 align:middle line:84%
integrate genomics with a
network model of biochemistry

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:00.400 align:middle line:90%
at many different levels.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.900 align:middle line:84%
Of course, there's
the genome encoding

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.580 align:middle line:90%
the components of that network.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:09.280 align:middle line:84%
But there's also the
systematic knockouts of genes

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.140 align:middle line:84%
and their effects
on the network.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.892 align:middle line:90%
Now, this slide is also review.

00:04:14.892 --> 00:04:16.600 align:middle line:84%
But it puts in context
where we'll mainly

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.375 align:middle line:90%
begin talking today.

00:04:18.375 --> 00:04:20.500 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk a little bit
about ordinary differential

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.540 align:middle line:84%
equations, both for--
at the beginning,

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:27.850 align:middle line:84%
under red blood cell, and in
the bistability discussion,

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.480 align:middle line:84%
how you can get, even with
just concentration and time,

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.700 align:middle line:84%
you can get these very
interesting behaviors,

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:35.290 align:middle line:90%
highly cooperative.

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:38.020 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll drop
concentration and time

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:39.560 align:middle line:84%
by a steady-state
approximation when

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.260 align:middle line:84%
we talk about flux balance
in the second half.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:45.220 align:middle line:84%
And of course, before,
we were talking

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.810 align:middle line:84%
about molecular mechanics in the
context of protein structure.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.790 align:middle line:84%
And master's
equations are the way

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:53.890 align:middle line:84%
of looking at stochastic
single molecules, which

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:57.490 align:middle line:90%
we'll mention in passing.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.920 align:middle line:84%
Eventually, in the
network, discussions

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.860 align:middle line:84%
will get to spatially
inhomogeneous models, where

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:07.090 align:middle line:84%
you actually care or realize
the importance of where

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:11.920 align:middle line:84%
particular molecules lie
in terms of their function.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:14.650 align:middle line:84%
Now, what are the
limits and problems

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:20.300 align:middle line:84%
in connecting the in
vitro parameters that

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:25.080 align:middle line:84%
were so key last time and this
time in developing a system

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:25.580 align:middle line:90%
model?

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:27.980 align:middle line:84%
By isolating
particular molecules,

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:32.600 align:middle line:84%
you can cut off, make
the system simpler.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:34.830 align:middle line:84%
But then there's the
problem of reintegration.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:38.180 align:middle line:84%
And here, this is more
historical artifact

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:42.350 align:middle line:84%
than really critical
to this discussion.

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:46.610 align:middle line:84%
And originally, enzyme kinetics
would ignore the products

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:49.530 align:middle line:84%
for technical
mathematical reasons.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.027 align:middle line:84%
But as you can see
from last time,

00:05:51.027 --> 00:05:53.360 align:middle line:84%
we showed that you could
represent those equations quite

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.380 align:middle line:84%
well and even make
the measurements.

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:58.130 align:middle line:84%
In the presence of
products, it would just

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:03.170 align:middle line:84%
be another few terms
in the rate equations.

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:08.875 align:middle line:84%
More critical, however, is that
including the product along

00:06:08.875 --> 00:06:11.000 align:middle line:84%
with substrate in the
measurements and the modeling

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.880 align:middle line:84%
is just the first step, because
how many different products

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:17.480 align:middle line:84%
and substrates and other
regulatory molecules

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:20.900 align:middle line:84%
might be involved that you
don't know about initially?

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:24.020 align:middle line:84%
In addition, the
conditions for doing

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:28.370 align:middle line:84%
the in vitro measurements,
it's hard to do them

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.620 align:middle line:90%
at the concentrations in cells.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:33.440 align:middle line:84%
And in cells, they're nearly
crystalline densities,

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:42.980 align:middle line:84%
as I say, up to 30% or
so, which is basically

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:47.600 align:middle line:84%
the very high concentration
of solute that occurs--

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.090 align:middle line:84%
proteins and other
macromolecules.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:54.980 align:middle line:84%
And the substrates,
the small molecules,

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:58.280 align:middle line:84%
are typically in vast excess
in in vitro reactions.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:00.810 align:middle line:84%
But they're very close
to equal molar in vivo

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.810 align:middle line:84%
because a lot of them are
bound up with enzymes.

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:04.352 align:middle line:84%
And you get interesting
observations,

00:07:04.352 --> 00:07:06.680 align:middle line:84%
such as this one mentioned
at the bottom where

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:09.620 align:middle line:84%
a chemical reaction, which is
spontaneous in solution, which

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:14.630 align:middle line:84%
is the epimerization
of galactose,

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:20.930 align:middle line:84%
does not occur in
normal cells of E. coli

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:24.020 align:middle line:84%
unless they have an
enzyme that catalyzes

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:25.920 align:middle line:84%
this normally
spontaneous reaction.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.730 align:middle line:84%
So this is curious that
it happens in solution.

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:31.955 align:middle line:84%
Doesn't happen in the cell
unless you have the enzyme.

00:07:31.955 --> 00:07:35.900 align:middle line:90%


00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:36.470 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.840 align:middle line:84%
So with all those
caveats in mind,

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:42.765 align:middle line:84%
and recognizing that even
though this model has more

00:07:42.765 --> 00:07:45.140 align:middle line:84%
measured parameters than almost
any other cellular model,

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.723 align:middle line:84%
they were all measured in vitro,
with the caveats that we just

00:07:47.723 --> 00:07:48.530 align:middle line:90%
mentioned.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:51.140 align:middle line:90%
And the cell is aimed--

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.390 align:middle line:84%
the function of
all these network

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:55.730 align:middle line:84%
is mainly to provide
the redox that

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:59.510 align:middle line:84%
keeps the hemoglobin
reduced and the ATP that

00:07:59.510 --> 00:08:02.255 align:middle line:84%
keeps the osmotic
pressure under control.

00:08:02.255 --> 00:08:06.350 align:middle line:90%


00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:08.360 align:middle line:84%
And also, the cell
is-- even though we've

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:10.540 align:middle line:84%
shown a little structure
in this network,

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:12.860 align:middle line:84%
the structure is
assumed to be fairly

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.140 align:middle line:84%
continuous within
the cell, which

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:18.660 align:middle line:84%
is a fairly good
approximation in this case.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.140 align:middle line:84%
So this is not
merely the stapling

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:25.340 align:middle line:84%
together of some kinetic
in vitro parameters.

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:33.610 align:middle line:84%
We have other considerations
in a real cell.

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:38.679 align:middle line:84%
We have the sort of
physical parameters

00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:44.290 align:middle line:84%
of the mass balance, energy
balance, and redox balance.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.510 align:middle line:84%
We've already mentioned
energy and redox.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:49.720 align:middle line:84%
But you have to have
conservation of mass,

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:52.810 align:middle line:84%
as we'll see develop quite a
bit more in the second half

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:54.910 align:middle line:90%
of this talk.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:58.780 align:middle line:84%
In addition, there are physics,
such as the osmotic pressure

00:08:58.780 --> 00:08:59.950 align:middle line:90%
and electron neutrality.

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:04.390 align:middle line:84%
There are cells which
do have transience

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:06.205 align:middle line:90%
of non-electrode neutrality.

00:09:06.205 --> 00:09:09.520 align:middle line:84%
But for the red blood cells,
certainly, one of the goals

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:12.130 align:middle line:84%
is to maintain very close
to electron neutrality

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:14.860 align:middle line:90%
and osmotically stable.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:17.110 align:middle line:84%
You want to have as many
non-adjustable constraints as

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:18.700 align:middle line:90%
possible.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:23.620 align:middle line:84%
As in other modeling systems, if
these are measurements, rather

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:26.440 align:middle line:84%
than adjustable
model parameters,

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:32.950 align:middle line:84%
then it allows you to test
the few hypotheses you have,

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:35.290 align:middle line:84%
and have them
overdetermined, and look

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:39.610 align:middle line:90%
for contradictions, outliers.

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:42.820 align:middle line:84%
And then, eventually,
we'll see advantages

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:45.010 align:middle line:84%
to knowing what the
maximum fluxes--

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:49.360 align:middle line:84%
the maximum rates that you can
have in these complex networks.

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:51.700 align:middle line:84%
And we could incorporate
gene regulation,

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:56.110 align:middle line:84%
as we've seen lots of
wholesale, increasingly

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:59.320 align:middle line:84%
accurate data on gene regulation
is becoming available.

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:01.930 align:middle line:84%
It would be nice
to integrate these,

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:04.662 align:middle line:84%
because the expression
of the proteins

00:10:04.662 --> 00:10:05.620 align:middle line:90%
affects their activity.

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:07.990 align:middle line:84%
The activity affects
these fluxes.

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:11.650 align:middle line:84%
So these fluxes are represented
here in slide number 8

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:15.880 align:middle line:84%
as dx dt, where each of
the x sub i's represents

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:18.910 align:middle line:84%
one of these blue dots, a
node in the network, where

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:22.900 align:middle line:84%
you have four basic processes
that affect it-- up to four.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:25.420 align:middle line:84%
You can have synthesis
steps, degradation steps.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:29.740 align:middle line:84%
So synthesis produces transport,
can bring it into the cell.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:31.000 align:middle line:90%
Degradation removes it.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:32.740 align:middle line:84%
And it can be
utilized, incorporated

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:34.120 align:middle line:90%
into the body of the cell.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.120 align:middle line:84%
And you can think of this
as a sink removing it

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:41.320 align:middle line:90%
from the free population of--

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:42.850 align:middle line:90%
for each x molecule.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:47.020 align:middle line:84%
And this can be restated as a
stoichiometric matrix [? so ?]

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:52.330 align:middle line:84%
ij, where that's mainly
1's and minus 1's.

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:57.580 align:middle line:84%
As you can see, in front
of each of these fluxes,

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:00.280 align:middle line:84%
synthesis degradation and so
forth will be 1's and minus

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:03.100 align:middle line:84%
1's that refer to
the stoichiometry,

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:05.980 align:middle line:90%
and sometimes 2's and 0's.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:09.175 align:middle line:84%
And then the transport
is its own vector.

00:11:09.175 --> 00:11:11.870 align:middle line:90%


00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:14.560 align:middle line:84%
And you'll see the utility
of the stoichiometric matrix

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:17.020 align:middle line:84%
where i is the
metabolite number,

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:20.260 align:middle line:84%
and j is the reaction
number or the enzyme number

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:25.790 align:middle line:84%
for all the possible reactions
that can occur in a cell.

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:27.290 align:middle line:84%
These can be all
possible reactions.

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:30.490 align:middle line:84%
Then you can toggle them on and
off with mutations or changing

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:32.530 align:middle line:90%
different cell types.

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:38.260 align:middle line:84%
Now, this particularly rich
system, the red blood cell,

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:42.280 align:middle line:84%
has been modeled many times and
continues to be modeled since

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:46.210 align:middle line:84%
the mid '70s and
now into the 2000s,

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:49.390 align:middle line:84%
starting originally
just with glycolysis,

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:53.890 align:middle line:84%
later adding pentose phosphate,
nucleotide metabolism,

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:59.260 align:middle line:84%
various [? pumps, ?]
[? osmotic ?] consideration.

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:02.860 align:middle line:84%
Hemoglobin ligands have been
treated from time to time,

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:07.450 align:middle line:84%
and less so issues having to
do with [INAUDIBLE] and shape.

00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:10.930 align:middle line:84%
No model includes all
of these in one model,

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:14.470 align:middle line:90%
although it's really very close.

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:18.340 align:middle line:84%
But there are models that
include all of the metabolism

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:22.240 align:middle line:84%
that we know and the
transport osmotic properties.

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:25.330 align:middle line:90%


00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:30.550 align:middle line:84%
Relatively few of those
were made available broadly.

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:34.360 align:middle line:84%
But now they're increasingly
being made available freely

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:39.550 align:middle line:90%
on the web, as models should be.

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:42.430 align:middle line:84%
The assumptions
behind this is that--

00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:45.970 align:middle line:90%


00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:47.890 align:middle line:84%
like I said, not
everything is modeled.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:51.370 align:middle line:84%
Some of it, for
mathematical convenience,

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:55.120 align:middle line:84%
you will typically, in order
to do differential equation--

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:57.490 align:middle line:84%
since there's vast
ranges of time constants,

00:12:57.490 --> 00:13:02.050 align:middle line:84%
from things that happen
extremely quickly to things

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:04.870 align:middle line:84%
that happen extremely slowly,
typically, what you do

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:09.550 align:middle line:84%
is you'll model with a window
in the middle, where you'll say,

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:11.110 align:middle line:84%
things that happened
very quickly

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.030 align:middle line:84%
can be treated as a
pseudo-equilibrium,

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:16.510 align:middle line:84%
as we've listed in this
middle line here on slide 10.

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:18.310 align:middle line:84%
Things that happen
extremely slowly

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:19.630 align:middle line:90%
can be treated as a constant.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:22.870 align:middle line:84%
If they happen over
the period of years,

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:26.020 align:middle line:84%
then in the course of an
experiment that might be hours,

00:13:26.020 --> 00:13:29.020 align:middle line:84%
they can be treated as
a constant or something

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:30.625 align:middle line:90%
that you systematically explore.

00:13:30.625 --> 00:13:34.450 align:middle line:90%


00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:38.500 align:middle line:84%
Although we'll typically
be ignoring little pieces

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:40.420 align:middle line:84%
of the metabolism, like
[INAUDIBLE] metabolism

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:40.920 align:middle line:90%
calcium--

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:44.290 align:middle line:90%


00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:47.080 align:middle line:84%
either as data comes
in from other systems

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:51.490 align:middle line:84%
and try to treat it by
homology or analogy.

00:13:51.490 --> 00:13:55.180 align:middle line:84%
In addition, when we talk
about a typical cell,

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:59.650 align:middle line:84%
this can mean that we assume
homogeneous distribution

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:03.040 align:middle line:84%
of molecules within the cell
and homogeneity from cell

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:05.170 align:middle line:84%
to cell within a
particular organism.

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:07.810 align:middle line:84%
It also means there's
a tendency to model

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:11.800 align:middle line:84%
a wild type without
respect to polymorphism.

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:15.160 align:middle line:84%
Although in human population,
with red blood cell function

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:18.160 align:middle line:84%
in particular, there's
quite a literature

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:24.115 align:middle line:84%
on mutations that affect the
functioning of the proteins

00:14:24.115 --> 00:14:25.240 align:middle line:90%
within the red blood cells.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:32.120 align:middle line:84%
Probably one of the best
studied human genetic systems.

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:33.890 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:14:33.890 --> 00:14:36.260 align:middle line:84%
Surface area is not
absolutely constant,

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:40.280 align:middle line:84%
although for the time
being, it's modeled as such.

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:42.590 align:middle line:84%
This is some examples
of a subset of it.

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:47.150 align:middle line:84%
This is a subset that
refers to glycolysis.

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:49.250 align:middle line:84%
You can see they all
have the same form where

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:52.100 align:middle line:84%
you have a change in a
concentration in moles

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:55.970 align:middle line:84%
per liter with respect to time
of some small molecule-- here

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:57.380 align:middle line:90%
glucose-6-phosphate.

00:14:57.380 --> 00:15:03.590 align:middle line:84%
It's some synthesis rate with
the subscript being hexokinase.

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:07.280 align:middle line:84%
This is the upper
left-hand corner.

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:11.480 align:middle line:84%
And then that's the
synthesis minus the sinks,

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:14.315 align:middle line:84%
the degradation rates, which
go through two other enzymes.

00:15:14.315 --> 00:15:20.540 align:middle line:90%


00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:22.170 align:middle line:84%
Again, a reminder--
at the bottom,

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:25.920 align:middle line:84%
you'll see this come up a
few times, just a reminder.

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:27.380 align:middle line:84%
Each of these is
a form of change

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:31.200 align:middle line:84%
in the concentration of some
small molecule with respect

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:31.820 align:middle line:90%
to time--

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:38.120 align:middle line:84%
is the sum of the synthesis,
subtracting the degradation

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:40.400 align:middle line:90%
transport utilization.

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:45.740 align:middle line:90%


00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:47.360 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:49.640 align:middle line:84%
Now, remember we focused
in on one little piece

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:51.410 align:middle line:90%
of this a couple of times now.

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:55.490 align:middle line:84%
This is a step that
happens to be allosteric.

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:58.223 align:middle line:84%
That means that, depending
on the concentration of the--

00:15:58.223 --> 00:16:08.653 align:middle line:90%


00:16:08.653 --> 00:16:10.070 align:middle line:84%
the top part of
this formula tends

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:13.230 align:middle line:84%
to be composed of substrates
and products having an effect.

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:17.090 align:middle line:84%
And then this term
in the denominator

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:21.440 align:middle line:84%
has a fourth power dependence
on a variety of other site

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:23.300 align:middle line:90%
effectors, including AMP.

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:28.250 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that the
velocity is either hyperbolic,

00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:32.840 align:middle line:84%
which is the upper curve--
which kind goes smoothly up

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:33.890 align:middle line:90%
and plateaus.

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:37.400 align:middle line:84%
While a sigmoid curve implies
greater cooperatively,

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:40.430 align:middle line:84%
which can be affected by some
of these other site effectors,

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:42.380 align:middle line:90%
which is more sigmoid in shape.

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:46.100 align:middle line:84%
And we'll use this
as a stepping stone

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:49.010 align:middle line:84%
to talking about what are
the various ways of getting

00:16:49.010 --> 00:16:50.325 align:middle line:90%
that sigmoid shape?

00:16:50.325 --> 00:16:51.950 align:middle line:84%
We've already talked
about how proteins

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:55.190 align:middle line:84%
can be multimers, dimers,
tetramers, and so forth.

00:16:55.190 --> 00:16:56.780 align:middle line:84%
That could be one
way of achieving

00:16:56.780 --> 00:17:00.080 align:middle line:84%
the sigmoid shape via
a conformation change

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:02.510 align:middle line:90%
which senses the second site.

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:06.349 align:middle line:84%
But we'll talk about another
way in just a couple of slides.

00:17:06.349 --> 00:17:11.090 align:middle line:84%
When we have the kinetic
expressions here,

00:17:11.090 --> 00:17:12.750 align:middle line:84%
they have the form
of the previous one.

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:17.510 align:middle line:84%
Most of them are simpler
than the one in slide 12.

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:20.420 align:middle line:84%
The model has a total
of 44 rate expressions.

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:24.060 align:middle line:84%
They have about five
constants, on average,

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:26.690 align:middle line:90%
so about 200 parameters.

00:17:26.690 --> 00:17:28.700 align:middle line:84%
These are not truly
adjustable in the sense

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:35.420 align:middle line:84%
that they're determined
from the in vitro reactions.

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:38.240 align:middle line:90%
What kind of assumptions?

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:40.820 align:middle line:84%
We've already mentioned the
difference between in vitro

00:17:40.820 --> 00:17:43.220 align:middle line:90%
and in vivo.

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:47.840 align:middle line:84%
We have the lingering
question of how many effectors

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:50.060 align:middle line:84%
might there be that
we don't know about?

00:17:50.060 --> 00:17:51.620 align:middle line:84%
Typically, these in
vitro experiments

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:54.470 align:middle line:84%
were done with a small number
of substrates and products

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:56.060 align:middle line:90%
that you know about.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:59.330 align:middle line:84%
But as a worst case scenario,
Mike Savageau you know,

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:02.150 align:middle line:84%
likes to trot out the glutamine
synthetase, which fortunately

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:04.160 align:middle line:90%
is not in this particular model.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:06.350 align:middle line:84%
But it could be that
there's an enzyme that's

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:08.720 align:middle line:84%
just as complicated but
hasn't been studied as much.

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:10.220 align:middle line:84%
In the case of
glutamine synthetase,

00:18:10.220 --> 00:18:11.510 align:middle line:90%
there are three substrates.

00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:13.430 align:middle line:84%
Remember, the previous
example, there

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:15.848 align:middle line:84%
were only two substrates
and two products.

00:18:15.848 --> 00:18:18.140 align:middle line:84%
Glutamine synthetase, three
substrates, three products,

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:20.600 align:middle line:84%
and has nine allosteric
effectors, rather than

00:18:20.600 --> 00:18:25.050 align:middle line:84%
the three or so in
the previous example.

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:26.840 align:middle line:84%
So this gives a
grand total of 15

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:28.820 align:middle line:84%
different molecules
you need to track.

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:33.710 align:middle line:84%
So the number of
different measurements

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:36.295 align:middle line:84%
you might have to
make, hypothetically--

00:18:36.295 --> 00:18:38.420 align:middle line:84%
no one's actually made this
number of measurements.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:41.930 align:middle line:84%
But Mike Savageau
likes to point out

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:44.330 align:middle line:84%
that even if you only
did four concentration

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:46.940 align:middle line:84%
points in this
multi-dimensional space,

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:48.830 align:middle line:84%
you have 4 to the
15th measurements,

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:51.260 align:middle line:90%
or a billion measurements.

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:53.420 align:middle line:84%
A billion isn't
nearly as intimidating

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:56.030 align:middle line:84%
as it was when he made
this statement in '76,

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:59.330 align:middle line:84%
but it still is not something
that's routinely done.

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:03.217 align:middle line:90%


00:19:03.217 --> 00:19:04.175 align:middle line:90%
What other constraints?

00:19:04.175 --> 00:19:06.110 align:middle line:84%
There are these physical
chemical constraints

00:19:06.110 --> 00:19:09.260 align:middle line:84%
of osmotic pressure and
electron neutrality,

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:11.240 align:middle line:84%
here stated a little
more explicitly.

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:14.690 align:middle line:90%
You have pi i equal to pi e.

00:19:14.690 --> 00:19:16.790 align:middle line:84%
That means the
pressure on the inside

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:18.575 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
pressure on the outside.

00:19:18.575 --> 00:19:20.450 align:middle line:84%
That sounds like a good
way to balance things

00:19:20.450 --> 00:19:22.280 align:middle line:84%
out so that the cell
doesn't explode.

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:27.050 align:middle line:84%
And explicitly, what that
means is these gas constant r

00:19:27.050 --> 00:19:31.820 align:middle line:84%
times the absolute
temperature, degrees Kelvin,

00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:40.070 align:middle line:84%
times the sum of the pressure
components for the j molecule,

00:19:40.070 --> 00:19:45.350 align:middle line:84%
going up to m chemical species,
for i, standing for Interior,

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:48.110 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the same
sum, equivalent sum

00:19:48.110 --> 00:19:51.650 align:middle line:84%
for the subscript
e, for Exterior.

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:54.260 align:middle line:84%
Electroneutrality has the
same set of concentrations

00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:59.240 align:middle line:84%
for the i, interior, and
j molecules, where now z

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:02.180 align:middle line:84%
is the charge, where charge
is the same z that we

00:20:02.180 --> 00:20:05.690 align:middle line:84%
had in m over z for
the mass spectrometry.

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:06.920 align:middle line:90%
So, OK.

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:11.690 align:middle line:84%
Now, some of the models
I give you today,

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:15.710 align:middle line:84%
we will compare it to the
calculated and the observed,

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:17.510 align:middle line:90%
as we have done before.

00:20:17.510 --> 00:20:19.730 align:middle line:84%
And here it's shown a
little bit differently

00:20:19.730 --> 00:20:22.490 align:middle line:84%
than how I've done it before
and how we'll do it later on.

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:25.388 align:middle line:84%
Typically, we would have
observed on one axis

00:20:25.388 --> 00:20:26.555 align:middle line:90%
and calculated on the other.

00:20:26.555 --> 00:20:30.375 align:middle line:90%


00:20:30.375 --> 00:20:32.000 align:middle line:84%
In general, we're
looking for outliers.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.580 align:middle line:84%
And here we're sorting by
the degree to which they

00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:37.800 align:middle line:90%
deviate from observation.

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:41.180 align:middle line:84%
And so the deviation is
going to be observation

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:43.580 align:middle line:90%
minus the calculated.

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:46.520 align:middle line:84%
And the degree of
deviation can either

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:49.070 align:middle line:84%
be normalized to the standard
deviation-- which is basically

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:51.350 align:middle line:84%
normalizing it to
how confident we are

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:53.420 align:middle line:90%
in the experimental measure.

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:56.310 align:middle line:84%
Or it can be normalized
to the averaged value,

00:20:56.310 --> 00:20:59.780 align:middle line:84%
which then becomes less
dependent upon the accuracy

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:01.730 align:middle line:84%
of the experiment
and more dependent

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:04.010 align:middle line:84%
upon-- with a fraction
of the-- and so we've

00:21:04.010 --> 00:21:05.780 align:middle line:90%
sorted it on the ladder.

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:07.910 align:middle line:84%
And you can see
that most of them

00:21:07.910 --> 00:21:09.510 align:middle line:90%
are less than two standard--

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:10.760 align:middle line:90%
sorry.

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:15.530 align:middle line:84%
Deviation is less than
twofold the average value

00:21:15.530 --> 00:21:18.350 align:middle line:84%
and less than seven
standard deviations

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:21.080 align:middle line:90%
in terms of the measurements.

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:23.840 align:middle line:84%
But the ones that are
furthest to the right

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:26.570 align:middle line:84%
are clearly the ones that
require the most attention,

00:21:26.570 --> 00:21:28.520 align:middle line:84%
either in the
experimental measurements

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:29.917 align:middle line:90%
or in the modeling.

00:21:29.917 --> 00:21:31.250 align:middle line:90%
These are steady-state measures.

00:21:31.250 --> 00:21:34.790 align:middle line:84%
This is kind of an abuse of
a beautiful kinetic model.

00:21:34.790 --> 00:21:39.800 align:middle line:84%
But it reflects the
limited data that exists.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:42.830 align:middle line:84%
And it's much easier to collect
steady-state data where,

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:44.420 align:middle line:90%
basically, the red blood cell--

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:46.400 align:middle line:84%
every particular molecule,
even though there

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:49.627 align:middle line:84%
are fluxes in and out of
every molecule, the molecule

00:21:49.627 --> 00:21:51.335 align:middle line:84%
concentration itself
is staying constant.

00:21:51.335 --> 00:21:53.970 align:middle line:90%


00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:55.730 align:middle line:90%
So that's how you--

00:21:55.730 --> 00:21:58.550 align:middle line:84%
if you're assuming each
molecular concentration

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:01.070 align:middle line:84%
is staying constant
with respect to time,

00:22:01.070 --> 00:22:04.680 align:middle line:84%
you're just measuring
steady-state levels.

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:06.920 align:middle line:84%
But if you're more
interested in looking

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:11.090 align:middle line:84%
at the dynamics, the movie
of how molecules will change

00:22:11.090 --> 00:22:16.400 align:middle line:84%
if you perturb the
system, you can

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:19.070 align:middle line:84%
think of a wide variety
of different curves

00:22:19.070 --> 00:22:21.440 align:middle line:90%
that the timecourses can take.

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:23.810 align:middle line:84%
And then the challenge is
how do you represent them?

00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:26.810 align:middle line:84%
You have 40-some
different small molecules.

00:22:26.810 --> 00:22:28.520 align:middle line:84%
You're tracking
the concentrations.

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:31.680 align:middle line:84%
And then the timecourse
can range over hours.

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:34.340 align:middle line:84%
But one way of doing
it is doing pairs

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:38.990 align:middle line:84%
of substrates at a
time, substrate a and b,

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:41.120 align:middle line:84%
and then monitoring the
timecourse as a vector.

00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:44.880 align:middle line:84%
Think of these as a series
of little points along here.

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:51.650 align:middle line:84%
And let's look in slide 17, look
in the upper left, number 1.

00:22:51.650 --> 00:22:55.610 align:middle line:84%
If, let's say, a
is converted to b,

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:58.380 align:middle line:84%
so that a plus b is
equal to some constant,

00:22:58.380 --> 00:23:03.260 align:middle line:84%
you can see this
slope of negative 1.

00:23:03.260 --> 00:23:07.040 align:middle line:84%
This is exactly negative 1,
because for every molecule

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:09.750 align:middle line:84%
of a that's consumed, a
molecule of b is produced.

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:12.620 align:middle line:84%
And so when you see this
perfect slope of negative 1,

00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:14.120 align:middle line:84%
then that's the
kind of relationship

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:18.770 align:middle line:84%
you expect between those
two, even if you're randomly

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:20.600 align:middle line:90%
sampling a dynamic system.

00:23:20.600 --> 00:23:23.750 align:middle line:90%
Here you're taking a timecourse.

00:23:23.750 --> 00:23:26.020 align:middle line:84%
Number two is a pair of
concentrations [? in the ?]

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:26.660 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:23:26.660 --> 00:23:28.130 align:middle line:84%
You'll get an
equilibrium constant,

00:23:28.130 --> 00:23:31.640 align:middle line:84%
and the ratios here
will not be negative 1.

00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:37.460 align:middle line:84%
They will be some constant which
determines that equilibrium.

00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:42.800 align:middle line:84%
You have two dynamically
independent metabolites,

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:45.530 align:middle line:90%
as in quadrant III here.

00:23:45.530 --> 00:23:51.740 align:middle line:84%
Basically, as you march
through increasing b,

00:23:51.740 --> 00:23:54.270 align:middle line:84%
a can stay constant because
it doesn't really care.

00:23:54.270 --> 00:23:57.950 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't respond to
changes in b or changes

00:23:57.950 --> 00:23:59.660 align:middle line:90%
that result in b changing.

00:23:59.660 --> 00:24:02.990 align:middle line:84%
And then maybe some
other set of dynamics

00:24:02.990 --> 00:24:06.260 align:middle line:84%
will cause a to change,
and b stays constant.

00:24:06.260 --> 00:24:08.130 align:middle line:84%
And if you sample
enough timecourse,

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:12.320 align:middle line:84%
you may find that this
fills up the entire space

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:17.750 align:middle line:84%
concentrations available to a
and b showing no correlation.

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:19.910 align:middle line:84%
Another interesting type
of phenomena you can see

00:24:19.910 --> 00:24:24.230 align:middle line:84%
is that not every possible
concentration of a--

00:24:24.230 --> 00:24:27.260 align:middle line:84%
and it's not completely
independent of b.

00:24:27.260 --> 00:24:30.170 align:middle line:84%
As you might start at a
particular point in a time

00:24:30.170 --> 00:24:32.690 align:middle line:84%
series at this gap here
in the lower right--

00:24:32.690 --> 00:24:36.320 align:middle line:84%
and you start decreasing
b and increasing a.

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:38.840 align:middle line:84%
And then, at some point, the
dynamics of the entire network,

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:43.160 align:middle line:84%
not just a and b, contribute
to now a taking a dive down

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:44.300 align:middle line:90%
and b increasing.

00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:46.820 align:middle line:84%
And eventually, you return
to that steady-state point,

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:48.590 align:middle line:84%
and you've described
all the conditions

00:24:48.590 --> 00:24:54.698 align:middle line:84%
that you might be able to
achieve in this closed loop.

00:24:54.698 --> 00:24:56.990 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to look at
these kind of phase diagrams.

00:24:56.990 --> 00:25:02.120 align:middle line:84%
The concentration of a
versus concentration of b--

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:05.240 align:middle line:84%
where a series of time points
that would be color coded.

00:25:05.240 --> 00:25:08.300 align:middle line:84%
These can be either lumped,
as they are in this diagram,

00:25:08.300 --> 00:25:12.140 align:middle line:84%
or in the next slide, we'll
see them separated out

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:13.192 align:middle line:90%
one metabolite at a time.

00:25:13.192 --> 00:25:14.900 align:middle line:84%
But it's the same
concept, whether you've

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:17.630 align:middle line:84%
got a group of metabolites
involved in glycolysis

00:25:17.630 --> 00:25:22.010 align:middle line:84%
lumped together, compared to,
say, a group that are redox.

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:26.562 align:middle line:84%
You have in the lower left
of each of these quadrants

00:25:26.562 --> 00:25:28.520 align:middle line:84%
is this time series that
we've been looking at.

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:32.270 align:middle line:84%
And the upper part is the
correlation coefficients,

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:35.510 align:middle line:84%
color coded so that blue
is a negative correlation,

00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:38.240 align:middle line:84%
and gray is very significant
positive correlation,

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:40.770 align:middle line:84%
and everything else is
something in between.

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:43.250 align:middle line:84%
So what you see from these is
you see, for example, here's

00:25:43.250 --> 00:25:46.130 align:middle line:84%
this up curve that's very
close to the negative 1 curve,

00:25:46.130 --> 00:25:48.860 align:middle line:84%
as if there's a
conservation reaction here

00:25:48.860 --> 00:25:52.130 align:middle line:84%
between glycolysis and
the adjacent steps.

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:55.500 align:middle line:84%
You see little
loops, for example,

00:25:55.500 --> 00:26:04.040 align:middle line:84%
in the lower left, adenine
biosynthesis, in that row,

00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:04.730 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:07.040 align:middle line:84%
You see examples of each
of these kind of behaviors

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:10.040 align:middle line:84%
here, where you're going
from the red point--

00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:11.540 align:middle line:90%
these little dots in red--

00:26:11.540 --> 00:26:16.820 align:middle line:84%
to green to blue to yellow to
the end gray in increasing time

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:20.540 align:middle line:84%
coarseness, starting with
0.1-hour resolution and ending

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:24.950 align:middle line:90%
with 300-hour resolution.

00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:26.360 align:middle line:84%
This is lumping,
where we're kind

00:26:26.360 --> 00:26:28.550 align:middle line:84%
of looking at things
like ATP and redox loads.

00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:31.130 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we look at it
one molecule at a time,

00:26:31.130 --> 00:26:32.750 align:middle line:84%
you obviously get
a more complex--

00:26:32.750 --> 00:26:35.990 align:middle line:84%
you get every possible pairwise
combination of molecules.

00:26:35.990 --> 00:26:38.120 align:middle line:84%
And you can see
these full dynamics.

00:26:38.120 --> 00:26:39.410 align:middle line:90%
Now, these are not data.

00:26:39.410 --> 00:26:40.520 align:middle line:90%
These are all simulations.

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:44.360 align:middle line:84%
And unfortunately, we don't have
that kind of dynamic resolution

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:46.602 align:middle line:90%
in experimental data just yet.

00:26:46.602 --> 00:26:47.810 align:middle line:90%
But this gives you some idea.

00:26:47.810 --> 00:26:50.780 align:middle line:84%
If you see some particularly
interesting phenomenon here,

00:26:50.780 --> 00:26:53.870 align:middle line:84%
then it might be a motivation
for going in and looking

00:26:53.870 --> 00:26:56.195 align:middle line:90%
at the data in more detail.

00:26:56.195 --> 00:26:58.730 align:middle line:90%


00:26:58.730 --> 00:27:01.210 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:27:01.210 --> 00:27:11.290 align:middle line:84%
Now, we mentioned this
difference between the ordinary

00:27:11.290 --> 00:27:16.810 align:middle line:84%
hyperbolic curve-- in the lower
right of this whole slide,

00:27:16.810 --> 00:27:18.790 align:middle line:90%
the upper left of the insert--

00:27:18.790 --> 00:27:20.650 align:middle line:84%
and the sigmoid
curve that you get

00:27:20.650 --> 00:27:26.810 align:middle line:90%
from an allosteric interaction.

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:30.190 align:middle line:84%
And within-- this
whole cell is set up

00:27:30.190 --> 00:27:33.250 align:middle line:84%
for transporting oxygen.
And as oxygen concentration

00:27:33.250 --> 00:27:36.550 align:middle line:84%
increases for
hemoglobin, you'll either

00:27:36.550 --> 00:27:40.360 align:middle line:84%
get this hyperbolic
or sigmoid curve

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:45.280 align:middle line:84%
as it gets increasingly sigmoid,
increasingly cooperative,

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:48.040 align:middle line:84%
with increasing
amounts of one of

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:52.780 align:middle line:84%
the intermediate metabolites,
2, 3 diphosphoglyserate.

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:55.090 align:middle line:84%
And you can see, this
is now the connection

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:57.760 align:middle line:90%
between the glycolytic pathway.

00:27:57.760 --> 00:28:01.450 align:middle line:84%
The regulation is sensing the
state of the glycolytic pathway

00:28:01.450 --> 00:28:05.013 align:middle line:90%
and the hemoglobin.

00:28:05.013 --> 00:28:06.430 align:middle line:84%
In addition, there
are connections

00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:08.890 align:middle line:84%
with the pH, which
is also regulated

00:28:08.890 --> 00:28:11.080 align:middle line:90%
in this, and the redox.

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:13.300 align:middle line:84%
You can see just above
it here, the hemoglobin

00:28:13.300 --> 00:28:16.700 align:middle line:84%
going to the unproductive
methemoglobin state.

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:18.670 align:middle line:84%
So you can see there
are connections

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:20.905 align:middle line:84%
in the network between this
ultimate function, which

00:28:20.905 --> 00:28:25.000 align:middle line:84%
is transporting oxygen, and all
these intermediate metabolism

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:25.810 align:middle line:90%
components.

00:28:25.810 --> 00:28:28.210 align:middle line:84%
And it also brings us
to this topic, again,

00:28:28.210 --> 00:28:31.900 align:middle line:84%
of this cooperativity and
how does it arise here?

00:28:31.900 --> 00:28:34.600 align:middle line:84%
And the hemoglobin has a
tetrameric conformation state

00:28:34.600 --> 00:28:37.900 align:middle line:84%
change, which is there's
a second site that

00:28:37.900 --> 00:28:44.750 align:middle line:84%
binds this organic
diphosphoglyserate.

00:28:44.750 --> 00:28:46.790 align:middle line:84%
But another direction
we can take--

00:28:46.790 --> 00:28:50.780 align:middle line:84%
so in the lower left-hand
corner of slide 21

00:28:50.780 --> 00:28:53.600 align:middle line:84%
is the same icon again of
how we get increasingly

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:57.890 align:middle line:84%
cooperative from hyperbolic to
sigmoidal, to the point where

00:28:57.890 --> 00:29:01.530 align:middle line:84%
this becomes almost vertical
and displaced from the origin

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:06.050 align:middle line:84%
so that the cell, at a
good point in response

00:29:06.050 --> 00:29:09.410 align:middle line:84%
to a stimulus, will
make a decision

00:29:09.410 --> 00:29:13.580 align:middle line:84%
to commit to the next
phase in cell division.

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:15.110 align:middle line:84%
This is similar to
the cell division

00:29:15.110 --> 00:29:17.300 align:middle line:84%
that we talked about
earlier in the context

00:29:17.300 --> 00:29:20.060 align:middle line:84%
of the microarray analysis
of yeast cell division.

00:29:20.060 --> 00:29:22.730 align:middle line:84%
Here we're talking about
a Xenopus amphibian

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:27.050 align:middle line:84%
oocyte, which has nice large
cells to do this kind of study.

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:30.050 align:middle line:84%
Where you need to
decide to come out of G1

00:29:30.050 --> 00:29:32.450 align:middle line:90%
and commit to synthesizing DNA--

00:29:32.450 --> 00:29:35.510 align:middle line:84%
in the S phase, in the
lower portion of the circle,

00:29:35.510 --> 00:29:37.100 align:middle line:84%
where you get, now,
two DNA molecules.

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:38.750 align:middle line:84%
And once you're
convinced that you've

00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:41.120 align:middle line:84%
finished replicating
all your DNA,

00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:44.030 align:middle line:84%
only at that point, then
can you commit to mitosis,

00:29:44.030 --> 00:29:47.240 align:middle line:90%
another major decision.

00:29:47.240 --> 00:29:49.130 align:middle line:84%
And then you get two
cells, and you go back,

00:29:49.130 --> 00:29:50.930 align:middle line:90%
and you complete the cell cycle.

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:52.940 align:middle line:84%
The little timecourse
at the bottom

00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:55.340 align:middle line:84%
here should be
reminiscent for you

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:57.530 align:middle line:84%
of the timecourse we
had of RNA synthesis

00:29:57.530 --> 00:29:59.810 align:middle line:90%
of various clusters of RNA.

00:29:59.810 --> 00:30:01.620 align:middle line:90%
Here it's the DNA synthesis.

00:30:01.620 --> 00:30:05.030 align:middle line:84%
You can see it ramps up in
the red S phase and then ramps

00:30:05.030 --> 00:30:08.670 align:middle line:84%
down in the metaphase due to
the creation of two new cells.

00:30:08.670 --> 00:30:13.730 align:middle line:84%
But we want to talk about how
do we make this as responsive

00:30:13.730 --> 00:30:16.970 align:middle line:84%
as, say, progesterone or
something that is signaling

00:30:16.970 --> 00:30:21.710 align:middle line:84%
cells that might be waiting
for long periods of time

00:30:21.710 --> 00:30:24.080 align:middle line:84%
to complete the next
step in a division

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:26.750 align:middle line:84%
cycle for an external
hormone stimulus

00:30:26.750 --> 00:30:31.955 align:middle line:84%
that it's time to start the
next step in cell cycle?

00:30:31.955 --> 00:30:33.830 align:middle line:84%
We want this to be
displaced from the origin.

00:30:33.830 --> 00:30:36.230 align:middle line:84%
You don't want it to be
just flipping on and off

00:30:36.230 --> 00:30:37.890 align:middle line:90%
irrespective of a stimulus.

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:40.830 align:middle line:84%
But when it does flip, you
want it to go very quickly.

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:43.100 align:middle line:90%
So how would we model this?

00:30:43.100 --> 00:30:49.130 align:middle line:84%
So look at the upper
right-hand diagram of slide 22.

00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:53.630 align:middle line:84%
And you have a set
of these oocytes kind

00:30:53.630 --> 00:30:57.230 align:middle line:84%
of diagrammatically
indicating their state.

00:30:57.230 --> 00:30:59.000 align:middle line:84%
Their state
determined by to what

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:07.190 align:middle line:84%
extent are they ready to commit
to the next division state?

00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:09.800 align:middle line:84%
Here we can think of
it as the biomarker

00:31:09.800 --> 00:31:14.270 align:middle line:84%
is the state of phosphorylation
of a protein MAP kinase.

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:17.240 align:middle line:84%
If it's phosphorylated,
then it's

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:18.410 align:middle line:90%
committed to this division.

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:21.530 align:middle line:84%
And we can think of this as the
black side of this gradient,

00:31:21.530 --> 00:31:22.910 align:middle line:90%
going from white to black.

00:31:22.910 --> 00:31:27.050 align:middle line:84%
But if you grind up a whole
population of these oocytes

00:31:27.050 --> 00:31:31.700 align:middle line:84%
and measure the total MAP kinase
phosphorylation as a function

00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:34.600 align:middle line:90%
of increasing stimulus, S--

00:31:34.600 --> 00:31:37.790 align:middle line:84%
in this case progesterone--
the response--

00:31:37.790 --> 00:31:39.560 align:middle line:84%
that is to say, the
phosphorylated state

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:42.440 align:middle line:90%
and the commitment to mitosis--

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:44.510 align:middle line:84%
will gradually
increase, as indicated

00:31:44.510 --> 00:31:47.060 align:middle line:90%
in the gradient model.

00:31:47.060 --> 00:31:49.010 align:middle line:84%
But that's if you
ground up all the cells.

00:31:49.010 --> 00:31:52.220 align:middle line:84%
But the other way of
obtaining that result

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:55.850 align:middle line:84%
would be if each cell is
making an all or none decision.

00:31:55.850 --> 00:31:58.820 align:middle line:84%
And what happens is the
probability of a cell being

00:31:58.820 --> 00:32:01.700 align:middle line:84%
in that all or none state
changes with increasing

00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:03.750 align:middle line:90%
stimulus to progesterone.

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:06.480 align:middle line:84%
And that is the
lower model and is,

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:10.400 align:middle line:84%
in fact, closer to reality,
as indicated by the experiment

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:16.610 align:middle line:84%
at the bottom part of the
slide here, where you have--

00:32:16.610 --> 00:32:18.727 align:middle line:84%
this is a part of a
concentration curve

00:32:18.727 --> 00:32:20.810 align:middle line:84%
where you're increasing
the stimulus progesterone.

00:32:20.810 --> 00:32:22.670 align:middle line:84%
But at this particular
stimulus, you

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:24.380 align:middle line:90%
can sample individual cells.

00:32:24.380 --> 00:32:27.650 align:middle line:84%
There's enough of the cell that
you can actually do proteomics

00:32:27.650 --> 00:32:29.090 align:middle line:90%
on individual cells.

00:32:29.090 --> 00:32:31.670 align:middle line:84%
And the proteomics
here is a western blot.

00:32:31.670 --> 00:32:36.050 align:middle line:84%
We mentioned this a
couple of lectures back.

00:32:36.050 --> 00:32:40.850 align:middle line:84%
And here you can see the two
states of the MAP kinase.

00:32:40.850 --> 00:32:42.950 align:middle line:84%
The phosphorylated
state is the slower.

00:32:42.950 --> 00:32:46.280 align:middle line:84%
Electrophoretically, it's the
upper band in this diagram.

00:32:46.280 --> 00:32:49.550 align:middle line:84%
And the lower band is the
unphosphorylated state.

00:32:49.550 --> 00:32:53.570 align:middle line:84%
And you can see there's no
example here of a cell which

00:32:53.570 --> 00:32:55.130 align:middle line:84%
is in an intermediate
state, where

00:32:55.130 --> 00:33:00.260 align:middle line:84%
it has half and half or 40/60
of the two different protein

00:33:00.260 --> 00:33:01.440 align:middle line:90%
forms.

00:33:01.440 --> 00:33:04.190 align:middle line:84%
However, if you, as
a thought experiment,

00:33:04.190 --> 00:33:07.370 align:middle line:84%
took all those cells and
mushed them up, and took this

00:33:07.370 --> 00:33:10.610 align:middle line:84%
and ran it all in one lane,
you would see a mixture.

00:33:10.610 --> 00:33:12.560 align:middle line:84%
You would see all the
intermediate states,

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:15.210 align:middle line:84%
and that would be a
function of progesterone.

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:18.980 align:middle line:84%
So this is a warning, similar
to ones that I've said before,

00:33:18.980 --> 00:33:23.780 align:middle line:84%
that when you grind everything
up and mix populations of cells

00:33:23.780 --> 00:33:26.780 align:middle line:84%
or molecules, you
need to be careful

00:33:26.780 --> 00:33:28.950 align:middle line:84%
because different cells
may be in different states,

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:30.908 align:middle line:84%
different molecules may
be in different states.

00:33:30.908 --> 00:33:34.580 align:middle line:84%
And the average behavior is
not the same as the individual.

00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:37.850 align:middle line:84%
But now, that's only
part of the lesson here.

00:33:37.850 --> 00:33:42.000 align:middle line:84%
The cells are going through
this all or none process.

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:44.900 align:middle line:84%
We can monitor it by
single-cell proteomics.

00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:46.700 align:middle line:90%
But how do we model it?

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:49.970 align:middle line:84%
Well, in very abstract
terms, the response

00:33:49.970 --> 00:33:53.090 align:middle line:84%
here can be modeled as in
this Hill coefficient, where

00:33:53.090 --> 00:33:57.950 align:middle line:84%
you have a stimulus, s, some
kinetic constant, k-- kind

00:33:57.950 --> 00:34:01.640 align:middle line:84%
of like a Michaelis constant--
where it's basically,

00:34:01.640 --> 00:34:09.409 align:middle line:84%
as gets s closer to k, it
has a larger response here,

00:34:09.409 --> 00:34:10.699 align:middle line:90%
effect on the response.

00:34:10.699 --> 00:34:17.420 align:middle line:84%
And that's nonlinear because
you have the exponent h.

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:20.929 align:middle line:84%
And the larger h is, the
more nonlinear it is.

00:34:20.929 --> 00:34:25.050 align:middle line:84%
So let's say that h is 1 in this
little schematic to the right

00:34:25.050 --> 00:34:25.550 align:middle line:90%
here.

00:34:25.550 --> 00:34:28.639 align:middle line:90%
It's hyperbolic.

00:34:28.639 --> 00:34:30.500 align:middle line:90%
No sigmoid character at all.

00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:33.418 align:middle line:84%
In the case of hemoglobin
and phosphofructokinase

00:34:33.418 --> 00:34:35.210 align:middle line:84%
that we talked about
in the red blood cell,

00:34:35.210 --> 00:34:42.800 align:middle line:84%
it's more sigmoidal, like an h
of 2.8, almost cube law there.

00:34:42.800 --> 00:34:45.830 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, even within
this system, one of the steps

00:34:45.830 --> 00:34:48.005 align:middle line:84%
that we'll talk about
in the next slide--

00:34:48.005 --> 00:34:51.359 align:middle line:90%


00:34:51.359 --> 00:34:54.530 align:middle line:84%
where you have a
stimulus of a Mos protein

00:34:54.530 --> 00:34:58.700 align:middle line:84%
and a response of this same
MAP kinase phosphorylation

00:34:58.700 --> 00:35:03.830 align:middle line:84%
response, it has a modest
sigmoid Hill coefficient of 3,

00:35:03.830 --> 00:35:05.390 align:middle line:90%
just like hemoglobin.

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:10.160 align:middle line:84%
But the overall response of
MAP kinase to progesterone

00:35:10.160 --> 00:35:14.510 align:middle line:90%
has an enormous exponent of 42.

00:35:14.510 --> 00:35:17.090 align:middle line:84%
That means it's almost
vertical, and it's

00:35:17.090 --> 00:35:19.230 align:middle line:90%
displaced from the origin.

00:35:19.230 --> 00:35:20.880 align:middle line:90%
How do we get that?

00:35:20.880 --> 00:35:21.380 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:35:21.380 --> 00:35:22.340 align:middle line:90%
So-- whoops.

00:35:22.340 --> 00:35:25.090 align:middle line:90%


00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:27.700 align:middle line:90%
So here is a proposed model.

00:35:27.700 --> 00:35:31.090 align:middle line:84%
And it's an interesting snapshot
in the inevitable evolution

00:35:31.090 --> 00:35:33.580 align:middle line:84%
of a model from something
very primitive, which

00:35:33.580 --> 00:35:38.950 align:middle line:84%
might have just been the
Mos effects on MAP kinase,

00:35:38.950 --> 00:35:42.470 align:middle line:84%
kind of as a direct effect
here, which as we said,

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:45.240 align:middle line:84%
has a Hill coefficient
of about threefold--

00:35:45.240 --> 00:35:46.580 align:middle line:90%
sorry.

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:49.150 align:middle line:84%
Hill coefficient
of 3, no threefold,

00:35:49.150 --> 00:35:51.010 align:middle line:90%
because that's an exponential.

00:35:51.010 --> 00:35:54.130 align:middle line:84%
But overall, combination
of two other factors

00:35:54.130 --> 00:35:56.680 align:middle line:84%
that you have a chain
of modifiers, each

00:35:56.680 --> 00:35:59.830 align:middle line:84%
close to saturation,
meaning that each one has

00:35:59.830 --> 00:36:03.640 align:middle line:90%
a slight sigmoid behavior.

00:36:03.640 --> 00:36:07.900 align:middle line:90%
And you've got-- sorry.

00:36:07.900 --> 00:36:09.370 align:middle line:84%
It could be a
hyperbolic function,

00:36:09.370 --> 00:36:12.640 align:middle line:84%
such as, let's just say,
this dotted line that's

00:36:12.640 --> 00:36:16.030 align:middle line:84%
going up smoothly from 0,
where it says "neither."

00:36:16.030 --> 00:36:18.100 align:middle line:84%
That would be the
effect of, if you just

00:36:18.100 --> 00:36:22.330 align:middle line:84%
have a normal enzymatic
reaction with no allostery,

00:36:22.330 --> 00:36:25.908 align:middle line:84%
no feedback, no
ultrasensitivity.

00:36:25.908 --> 00:36:27.700 align:middle line:84%
If each of those has
a component and you're

00:36:27.700 --> 00:36:30.980 align:middle line:84%
close to saturation-- meaning
your substrate is very high.

00:36:30.980 --> 00:36:33.380 align:middle line:84%
And so you're going as
fast as the enzyme will go.

00:36:33.380 --> 00:36:34.660 align:middle line:90%
You have a chain of those.

00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:37.450 align:middle line:84%
You can show through
kinetic modeling

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:41.080 align:middle line:84%
that that will create a high
sensitivity to the reaction.

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:46.840 align:middle line:84%
And that's what the furthest
dotted curve close to the axis

00:36:46.840 --> 00:36:49.660 align:middle line:90%
is, ultrasensitivity alone.

00:36:49.660 --> 00:36:52.660 align:middle line:84%
And then, here, you
have progesterone

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:59.740 align:middle line:84%
going in, in the upper left,
affecting a complicated rate.

00:36:59.740 --> 00:37:02.470 align:middle line:84%
Now, these rate constants
don't necessarily

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:07.188 align:middle line:84%
mean a unitary,
simple kinetic step.

00:37:07.188 --> 00:37:08.980 align:middle line:84%
They can represent
something as complicated

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:13.840 align:middle line:84%
as going from Amino Acids, AA,
to a particular protein, Mos.

00:37:13.840 --> 00:37:17.080 align:middle line:84%
And then the reverse
reaction, k sub minus 1,

00:37:17.080 --> 00:37:19.420 align:middle line:84%
is the degradation of Mos
back to amino acids, which

00:37:19.420 --> 00:37:24.790 align:middle line:84%
is not a reverse of the same
enzyme set by any means.

00:37:24.790 --> 00:37:27.260 align:middle line:90%
The next step, k2, is simpler.

00:37:27.260 --> 00:37:30.010 align:middle line:84%
It's just Mos being
phosphorylated by, actually,

00:37:30.010 --> 00:37:34.960 align:middle line:84%
our friend MAP kinase in
its [? phosphorous ?] state,

00:37:34.960 --> 00:37:37.960 align:middle line:90%
producing the--

00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:41.950 align:middle line:84%
for catalyzing the Mos
phosphorylation, which

00:37:41.950 --> 00:37:43.630 align:middle line:84%
catalyzes another
phosphorylation

00:37:43.630 --> 00:37:46.990 align:middle line:84%
of another protein, which
then positively stimulates

00:37:46.990 --> 00:37:48.830 align:middle line:90%
MAP kinase itself.

00:37:48.830 --> 00:37:51.460 align:middle line:84%
So this whole thing is a
set of positive reactions.

00:37:51.460 --> 00:37:54.760 align:middle line:84%
Each of the
phosphoproteins increases

00:37:54.760 --> 00:38:00.850 align:middle line:84%
the enzymatic tendency for each
of the other phosphoproteins

00:38:00.850 --> 00:38:01.810 align:middle line:90%
to be produced.

00:38:01.810 --> 00:38:04.060 align:middle line:84%
So you can see that this is
kind of on a hair trigger.

00:38:04.060 --> 00:38:06.143 align:middle line:84%
If any one of these
phosphoproteins gets produced,

00:38:06.143 --> 00:38:08.230 align:middle line:84%
then it'll increase
all the other ones,

00:38:08.230 --> 00:38:11.680 align:middle line:84%
and it'll be a very
cooperative procedure.

00:38:11.680 --> 00:38:15.100 align:middle line:84%
And that's what this, furthest
towards the axis in the lower

00:38:15.100 --> 00:38:16.150 align:middle line:90%
left-hand corner--

00:38:16.150 --> 00:38:18.700 align:middle line:84%
where you have this
positive feedback

00:38:18.700 --> 00:38:23.410 align:middle line:84%
alone causes this
very great tendency

00:38:23.410 --> 00:38:29.470 align:middle line:84%
to just jump up from 0
to very high response

00:38:29.470 --> 00:38:31.640 align:middle line:90%
with very little stimulus.

00:38:31.640 --> 00:38:32.860 align:middle line:90%
Well, that's dangerous.

00:38:32.860 --> 00:38:34.960 align:middle line:84%
You want it to be
nearly vertical,

00:38:34.960 --> 00:38:37.300 align:middle line:84%
but you don't want to be
nearly vertical at 0 stimulus,

00:38:37.300 --> 00:38:38.740 align:middle line:90%
because that's unstable.

00:38:38.740 --> 00:38:40.022 align:middle line:90%
So you want to move it over.

00:38:40.022 --> 00:38:41.980 align:middle line:84%
And that's where the
ultrasensitivity comes in,

00:38:41.980 --> 00:38:44.860 align:middle line:84%
when you have this chain of
modifiers putting both together

00:38:44.860 --> 00:38:49.810 align:middle line:84%
as a solid black line, where
it's shifted over so you have--

00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:54.340 align:middle line:84%
this assay was done with Mos,
rather than progesterone,

00:38:54.340 --> 00:38:54.940 align:middle line:90%
as the input.

00:38:54.940 --> 00:38:57.460 align:middle line:90%


00:38:57.460 --> 00:39:03.370 align:middle line:84%
But you can see the overall
increased cooperativity

00:39:03.370 --> 00:39:07.460 align:middle line:90%
and shifting to the right.

00:39:07.460 --> 00:39:10.210 align:middle line:84%
So that's an example of how
you can get this very high Hill

00:39:10.210 --> 00:39:13.630 align:middle line:84%
coefficient and where
you can get bistability

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:15.670 align:middle line:90%
without stochastic.

00:39:15.670 --> 00:39:18.280 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine that you can
have stochastic bistability.

00:39:18.280 --> 00:39:20.950 align:middle line:84%
If you have one molecule in
the cell, and either it's there

00:39:20.950 --> 00:39:23.115 align:middle line:84%
or it's not, then
you have bistability.

00:39:23.115 --> 00:39:24.490 align:middle line:84%
You have two states
for the cell.

00:39:24.490 --> 00:39:27.160 align:middle line:84%
Either it's the cell with
the molecule or without.

00:39:27.160 --> 00:39:30.160 align:middle line:84%
But here you can see that
even with a very large cell--

00:39:30.160 --> 00:39:32.410 align:middle line:84%
Xenopus oocytes being one
of the largest cells--

00:39:32.410 --> 00:39:34.480 align:middle line:84%
and very large
amounts of proteins--

00:39:34.480 --> 00:39:37.720 align:middle line:84%
enough that you can easily
see them in proteomics--

00:39:37.720 --> 00:39:39.520 align:middle line:84%
you can still
achieve bistability

00:39:39.520 --> 00:39:40.930 align:middle line:90%
with the right kinetic model.

00:39:40.930 --> 00:39:43.990 align:middle line:84%
Not every random model
would achieve that high Hill

00:39:43.990 --> 00:39:45.760 align:middle line:90%
coefficient.

00:39:45.760 --> 00:39:46.270 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:39:46.270 --> 00:39:47.950 align:middle line:84%
So we're just going
to briefly mention

00:39:47.950 --> 00:39:49.790 align:middle line:84%
the other way of
getting bistability,

00:39:49.790 --> 00:39:54.050 align:middle line:84%
which is via stochastic,
so small molecules.

00:39:54.050 --> 00:39:59.510 align:middle line:90%
And here, an example--

00:39:59.510 --> 00:40:01.540 align:middle line:84%
so instead of dealing
with very large cells

00:40:01.540 --> 00:40:03.850 align:middle line:84%
with very large numbers
of molecules involved,

00:40:03.850 --> 00:40:07.090 align:middle line:84%
here in, say, bacteria
in particular,

00:40:07.090 --> 00:40:11.200 align:middle line:84%
a phage-infected
bacteria, you generally

00:40:11.200 --> 00:40:14.290 align:middle line:84%
have the case of very
small bursts of activity.

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:18.550 align:middle line:84%
A transcription factor
will bind to a promoter.

00:40:18.550 --> 00:40:20.830 align:middle line:84%
Before the transcription
factor comes off,

00:40:20.830 --> 00:40:24.390 align:middle line:84%
it may cause a small burst of
a couple of RNA transcripts

00:40:24.390 --> 00:40:27.720 align:middle line:84%
being made by a couple
of RNA polymerases

00:40:27.720 --> 00:40:29.460 align:middle line:84%
seeing those
transcription factors.

00:40:29.460 --> 00:40:32.430 align:middle line:84%
Then each of those RNAs
caused its own bursts

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:34.680 align:middle line:84%
of protein synthesis where
a whole series of ribosomes

00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:36.870 align:middle line:90%
will bind in a polysome.

00:40:36.870 --> 00:40:41.220 align:middle line:84%
And you'll get this double
burst of RNA and protein.

00:40:41.220 --> 00:40:44.850 align:middle line:84%
And the stochastic binding
of that transcription factor

00:40:44.850 --> 00:40:50.850 align:middle line:84%
that starts that burst can
be modeled by reasonably

00:40:50.850 --> 00:40:53.370 align:middle line:84%
measured parameters for
each of these steps.

00:40:53.370 --> 00:40:55.620 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that
cells one, two, and three,

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:58.380 align:middle line:90%
in the lower left of slide 25--

00:40:58.380 --> 00:41:00.510 align:middle line:84%
where time is the
horizontal axis up to, say,

00:41:00.510 --> 00:41:04.440 align:middle line:84%
45 minutes or a cell
division or two.

00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:08.070 align:middle line:84%
While the number of
product proteins here,

00:41:08.070 --> 00:41:11.370 align:middle line:84%
measured in dimers of
protein, fluctuates,

00:41:11.370 --> 00:41:14.700 align:middle line:84%
where cell one gets an early
start, early burst, and cell

00:41:14.700 --> 00:41:17.880 align:middle line:84%
three hasn't quite
hit its burst yet.

00:41:17.880 --> 00:41:19.630 align:middle line:84%
So you can see there's
a lot of variation.

00:41:19.630 --> 00:41:22.560 align:middle line:84%
And this is one way of
achieving a bistable switch.

00:41:22.560 --> 00:41:24.240 align:middle line:84%
But as you've seen--
not the only way.

00:41:24.240 --> 00:41:27.090 align:middle line:84%
You can also do it where
all the proteins are

00:41:27.090 --> 00:41:28.920 align:middle line:90%
present in all large amounts.

00:41:28.920 --> 00:41:31.085 align:middle line:84%
If you do choose to go
the stochastic route--

00:41:31.085 --> 00:41:33.460 align:middle line:84%
and this might be an interesting
project for some of you.

00:41:33.460 --> 00:41:36.210 align:middle line:84%
It's by no means shown
to be mission critical

00:41:36.210 --> 00:41:41.640 align:middle line:84%
for the community
of systems modelers.

00:41:41.640 --> 00:41:47.160 align:middle line:84%
But many people believe
it is a way to go.

00:41:47.160 --> 00:41:49.950 align:middle line:84%
There has been great
progress since 1977

00:41:49.950 --> 00:41:57.960 align:middle line:84%
when Gillespie proposed the
algorithm named after him

00:41:57.960 --> 00:42:01.260 align:middle line:84%
for stochastic simulation,
a couple of chemical

00:42:01.260 --> 00:42:04.080 align:middle line:84%
reactions in general, not
just biological, biochemical

00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:05.340 align:middle line:90%
reactions.

00:42:05.340 --> 00:42:11.370 align:middle line:84%
Since then, Gibson and
Bruck, within the last couple

00:42:11.370 --> 00:42:14.460 align:middle line:84%
of years, have come up with
an algorithm, which is now

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:16.320 align:middle line:84%
time proportional to
the logarithm of number

00:42:16.320 --> 00:42:18.403 align:middle line:84%
of reactions, rather than
the number of reactions.

00:42:18.403 --> 00:42:22.810 align:middle line:84%
Any time you go from n to
log n, this is big progress.

00:42:22.810 --> 00:42:27.900 align:middle line:84%
And this is done by better
tracking of calculations

00:42:27.900 --> 00:42:30.360 align:middle line:90%
that you can reuse.

00:42:30.360 --> 00:42:32.550 align:middle line:84%
And so I encourage
you to take a look

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:36.060 align:middle line:90%
at this aspect of stochastics.

00:42:36.060 --> 00:42:39.900 align:middle line:84%
Another aspect is people
often think of the stochastics

00:42:39.900 --> 00:42:40.920 align:middle line:90%
as kind of a nuisance.

00:42:40.920 --> 00:42:42.570 align:middle line:84%
They increase the
computer time that it

00:42:42.570 --> 00:42:44.160 align:middle line:90%
takes to do simulations.

00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:47.130 align:middle line:84%
They increase your uncertainty
about the simulations

00:42:47.130 --> 00:42:48.990 align:middle line:90%
that you then produce.

00:42:48.990 --> 00:42:50.940 align:middle line:84%
But there is an
aspect of it which

00:42:50.940 --> 00:42:52.410 align:middle line:84%
is just beginning
to be harnessed

00:42:52.410 --> 00:42:54.330 align:middle line:84%
in various fields
of engineering,

00:42:54.330 --> 00:42:57.130 align:middle line:84%
and biological engineering
is no exception.

00:42:57.130 --> 00:43:00.390 align:middle line:84%
And I give you two examples
here to just whet your appetite.

00:43:00.390 --> 00:43:02.070 align:middle line:84%
Again, I'm not going
to go through them.

00:43:02.070 --> 00:43:04.830 align:middle line:84%
But you can see that
you can actually

00:43:04.830 --> 00:43:08.460 align:middle line:84%
make switches and amplifiers
for gene expression-- gene

00:43:08.460 --> 00:43:10.270 align:middle line:84%
expression being one
of our favorite topics

00:43:10.270 --> 00:43:12.450 align:middle line:84%
in this course-- which
are based on noise

00:43:12.450 --> 00:43:16.170 align:middle line:84%
and where you can
get bistability

00:43:16.170 --> 00:43:19.770 align:middle line:90%
using these fluctuations.

00:43:19.770 --> 00:43:21.718 align:middle line:84%
And that's not too
unexpected based

00:43:21.718 --> 00:43:23.010 align:middle line:90%
on what we've just been saying.

00:43:23.010 --> 00:43:24.990 align:middle line:84%
But in addition, you
can get stochastic

00:43:24.990 --> 00:43:30.090 align:middle line:84%
focusing where the fluctuation
allows enhanced sensitivity.

00:43:30.090 --> 00:43:31.380 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:43:31.380 --> 00:43:33.480 align:middle line:84%
So I encourage you
to look at that.

00:43:33.480 --> 00:43:34.950 align:middle line:84%
Now, a particular
place where you

00:43:34.950 --> 00:43:37.980 align:middle line:84%
might worry that stochastics
is coming into play quite a bit

00:43:37.980 --> 00:43:39.780 align:middle line:84%
is in chromosome copy
number, whether this

00:43:39.780 --> 00:43:42.420 align:middle line:84%
is eukaryotic chromosomes or
in the case we'll illustrate,

00:43:42.420 --> 00:43:45.602 align:middle line:84%
a very simple case of
plasmid chromosomes.

00:43:45.602 --> 00:43:47.310 align:middle line:84%
Now, the interesting
thing about plasmids

00:43:47.310 --> 00:43:52.440 align:middle line:84%
is they can either be in
lockstep with cell division,

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:53.940 align:middle line:84%
the way that
eukaryotic chromosomes

00:43:53.940 --> 00:43:56.398 align:middle line:84%
are-- in the cases of Xenopus
oocytes we just talked about,

00:43:56.398 --> 00:43:58.530 align:middle line:90%
where it makes a big decision.

00:43:58.530 --> 00:44:00.420 align:middle line:84%
And that's the case
of the R1 plasmids.

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:04.080 align:middle line:84%
Or it can be more of kind
of a cloud of copy number,

00:44:04.080 --> 00:44:08.010 align:middle line:84%
where they're trying to be
close to a target number

00:44:08.010 --> 00:44:11.590 align:middle line:84%
where you'll have more
copies than one per cell.

00:44:11.590 --> 00:44:15.330 align:middle line:84%
And so as the cell divides,
it kind of randomly

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:21.180 align:middle line:84%
takes a partition of
that number of plasmids.

00:44:21.180 --> 00:44:24.130 align:middle line:90%
And ColE1 is an example of that.

00:44:24.130 --> 00:44:26.730 align:middle line:84%
And you model it in
order to determine

00:44:26.730 --> 00:44:29.970 align:middle line:90%
the factors that govern it.

00:44:29.970 --> 00:44:31.860 align:middle line:90%
This has implication.

00:44:31.860 --> 00:44:36.450 align:middle line:84%
The copy number will affect
the expression levels.

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:40.530 align:middle line:84%
And the expression levels are
of importance to biotechnology.

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:43.840 align:middle line:84%
And plasmids are, of course,
also important in pathogenesis.

00:44:43.840 --> 00:44:47.010 align:middle line:84%
The copy number
affects pathogenesis

00:44:47.010 --> 00:44:49.590 align:middle line:84%
since plasmids are a major
way that drug resistance

00:44:49.590 --> 00:44:51.130 align:middle line:90%
elements are passed around.

00:44:51.130 --> 00:44:55.170 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a look
at one, hopefully,

00:44:55.170 --> 00:44:58.170 align:middle line:84%
highly oversimplified
version of this.

00:44:58.170 --> 00:45:01.530 align:middle line:84%
Here you have two
RNAs, imaginatively

00:45:01.530 --> 00:45:03.870 align:middle line:90%
called RNA 1 and RNA 2.

00:45:03.870 --> 00:45:08.220 align:middle line:84%
We'll start-- RNA 2
is transcribed here,

00:45:08.220 --> 00:45:10.515 align:middle line:84%
on the bottom strand,
from right to left.

00:45:10.515 --> 00:45:13.560 align:middle line:90%


00:45:13.560 --> 00:45:16.230 align:middle line:84%
So actually, look at the
very bottom of slide 30.

00:45:16.230 --> 00:45:21.060 align:middle line:84%
The magenta RNA polymerase
is making RNA 2.

00:45:21.060 --> 00:45:27.910 align:middle line:84%
And if nothing binds to RNA 2,
if RNA 1 does not bind to it,

00:45:27.910 --> 00:45:29.290 align:middle line:90%
RNase H will cleave it.

00:45:29.290 --> 00:45:31.420 align:middle line:84%
It will then bind to
blue DNA polymerase

00:45:31.420 --> 00:45:34.840 align:middle line:84%
and will start
replicating the plasmid.

00:45:34.840 --> 00:45:37.750 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, if RNA 1,
which is made on the opposite

00:45:37.750 --> 00:45:40.510 align:middle line:84%
strand of RNA 2-- it's
this antisense story.

00:45:40.510 --> 00:45:44.110 align:middle line:84%
It will then come and bind
to RNA 2, sort of in trans.

00:45:44.110 --> 00:45:46.150 align:middle line:84%
It acts as a
transacting inhibitor.

00:45:46.150 --> 00:45:49.300 align:middle line:84%
It's aided and abetted
by the Rom protein.

00:45:49.300 --> 00:45:51.970 align:middle line:84%
And now you don't get
cleavage of RNase H.

00:45:51.970 --> 00:45:54.670 align:middle line:84%
And so DNA polymerase
doesn't have a primer

00:45:54.670 --> 00:45:57.190 align:middle line:84%
upon which to act, and
you don't get replication.

00:45:57.190 --> 00:46:00.260 align:middle line:84%
And this is, of course,
not just a yes or no thing.

00:46:00.260 --> 00:46:02.380 align:middle line:84%
This is something that's
regulated and allows

00:46:02.380 --> 00:46:04.870 align:middle line:84%
it to feed back to get
the right copy number.

00:46:04.870 --> 00:46:07.210 align:middle line:84%
You don't want it to get
an infinite copy number,

00:46:07.210 --> 00:46:10.330 align:middle line:84%
or else it'll choke the
cell that's harboring it.

00:46:10.330 --> 00:46:12.410 align:middle line:84%
But you don't want it
to drop down low enough

00:46:12.410 --> 00:46:14.080 align:middle line:84%
so that then many
cells will segregate

00:46:14.080 --> 00:46:19.420 align:middle line:90%
with no copies of the plasmid.

00:46:19.420 --> 00:46:20.920 align:middle line:84%
So you want to have
a mass balance.

00:46:20.920 --> 00:46:24.340 align:middle line:84%
You have to have
conservation of mass.

00:46:24.340 --> 00:46:30.460 align:middle line:84%
You want to be able to
model both the initiation

00:46:30.460 --> 00:46:32.270 align:middle line:90%
and degradation and inhibition.

00:46:32.270 --> 00:46:34.750 align:middle line:84%
So you do this by making
some simplifications

00:46:34.750 --> 00:46:36.730 align:middle line:90%
that the RNase H rate is fast.

00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:38.800 align:middle line:84%
Remember we had slow
and fast reactions

00:46:38.800 --> 00:46:46.090 align:middle line:84%
that we would eliminate, so too
with the DNA polymerization.

00:46:46.090 --> 00:46:49.930 align:middle line:84%
By subsuming the RNA 2
concentration in an RNA

00:46:49.930 --> 00:46:51.860 align:middle line:84%
1-based model, you
can simplify it

00:46:51.860 --> 00:46:54.310 align:middle line:84%
so that you're really only
considering two species, RNA

00:46:54.310 --> 00:46:56.590 align:middle line:90%
1 and the plasmid DNA itself.

00:46:56.590 --> 00:47:02.420 align:middle line:84%
We'll call these r and n for
the two different molecules.

00:47:02.420 --> 00:47:04.900 align:middle line:84%
So this is just a
way of introducing

00:47:04.900 --> 00:47:06.100 align:middle line:90%
a two-species model.

00:47:06.100 --> 00:47:11.800 align:middle line:84%
We're going to come up with
a rate equation for change

00:47:11.800 --> 00:47:13.670 align:middle line:90%
of RNA 1 with respect to time.

00:47:13.670 --> 00:47:16.010 align:middle line:84%
And then the next slide
will show the change

00:47:16.010 --> 00:47:17.800 align:middle line:84%
in the concentration
of the plasmid.

00:47:17.800 --> 00:47:20.590 align:middle line:90%
Concentration of the RNA is r.

00:47:20.590 --> 00:47:22.540 align:middle line:84%
Concentration of
the plasmid is n.

00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:25.130 align:middle line:90%
We have dr dt and dn dt.

00:47:25.130 --> 00:47:26.240 align:middle line:90%
And this is very simple.

00:47:26.240 --> 00:47:27.698 align:middle line:84%
It's just like what
we were talking

00:47:27.698 --> 00:47:30.250 align:middle line:90%
about with the metabolites.

00:47:30.250 --> 00:47:32.620 align:middle line:90%
You synthesize the RNA.

00:47:32.620 --> 00:47:33.610 align:middle line:90%
That's a positive term.

00:47:33.610 --> 00:47:35.860 align:middle line:84%
You degrade it, or
you dilute it out.

00:47:35.860 --> 00:47:37.840 align:middle line:84%
The dilution is based
on the growth rate.

00:47:37.840 --> 00:47:39.160 align:middle line:90%
Mu is a typical--

00:47:39.160 --> 00:47:41.753 align:middle line:84%
it's used in growth rates
in population genetics,

00:47:41.753 --> 00:47:43.420 align:middle line:84%
and here it's used
in chemical kinetics.

00:47:43.420 --> 00:47:45.130 align:middle line:84%
In fact, this is,
in a certain sense,

00:47:45.130 --> 00:47:47.290 align:middle line:84%
an example of a very
exciting field where you're

00:47:47.290 --> 00:47:53.470 align:middle line:84%
bringing together population
genetics and chemical

00:47:53.470 --> 00:47:54.715 align:middle line:90%
kinetics into one place.

00:47:54.715 --> 00:47:57.370 align:middle line:90%


00:47:57.370 --> 00:48:00.160 align:middle line:84%
And population genetics
and chemical kinetics,

00:48:00.160 --> 00:48:04.180 align:middle line:84%
when they come together,
unite some of the most

00:48:04.180 --> 00:48:06.650 align:middle line:90%
disparate parts of this course.

00:48:06.650 --> 00:48:07.150 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:48:07.150 --> 00:48:08.890 align:middle line:84%
So now we've got
an equation here

00:48:08.890 --> 00:48:13.180 align:middle line:84%
where the positive term is
k1, the rate of initiation

00:48:13.180 --> 00:48:15.220 align:middle line:90%
of RNA synthesis.

00:48:15.220 --> 00:48:18.638 align:middle line:84%
And it's, of course,
the more molecules, n,

00:48:18.638 --> 00:48:20.680 align:middle line:84%
the higher the concentration
of the plasmid, then

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:21.850 align:middle line:84%
the more RNA you're
going to make.

00:48:21.850 --> 00:48:22.990 align:middle line:84%
So that makes
sense that you have

00:48:22.990 --> 00:48:25.690 align:middle line:84%
the product, the rate constant
times the number of plasmids.

00:48:25.690 --> 00:48:27.670 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, the
loss of it is going

00:48:27.670 --> 00:48:29.170 align:middle line:84%
to be related to
the number of RNAs.

00:48:29.170 --> 00:48:31.630 align:middle line:84%
More RNAs you have,
the more that you're

00:48:31.630 --> 00:48:34.000 align:middle line:84%
going to lose, more
that you have to lose.

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:36.580 align:middle line:90%


00:48:36.580 --> 00:48:40.420 align:middle line:84%
So that's the RNA, and
this is for the DNA.

00:48:40.420 --> 00:48:47.470 align:middle line:84%
Here you have
dependence on the RNA.

00:48:47.470 --> 00:48:49.900 align:middle line:84%
RNA 1, remember, is
the thing that we

00:48:49.900 --> 00:48:51.430 align:middle line:90%
modeled in the previous slide--

00:48:51.430 --> 00:48:53.200 align:middle line:90%
is an inhibitor.

00:48:53.200 --> 00:48:58.240 align:middle line:84%
And so it's going to,
when it binds to RNA 2--

00:48:58.240 --> 00:49:00.380 align:middle line:84%
which is implicitly
modeled here--

00:49:00.380 --> 00:49:03.400 align:middle line:84%
it's going to have this
inhibitory constant

00:49:03.400 --> 00:49:05.600 align:middle line:90%
in the denominator.

00:49:05.600 --> 00:49:08.380 align:middle line:84%
And so as the
inhibitor RNA goes up,

00:49:08.380 --> 00:49:14.020 align:middle line:84%
this inhibitory term goes up,
the forward rate goes down.

00:49:14.020 --> 00:49:17.530 align:middle line:84%
And the rate of
replication is going

00:49:17.530 --> 00:49:19.570 align:middle line:84%
to be also dependent
on plasmid copy number,

00:49:19.570 --> 00:49:21.550 align:middle line:90%
so it goes up within.

00:49:21.550 --> 00:49:25.070 align:middle line:84%
The dilution rate is, of
course, dependent on n as well.

00:49:25.070 --> 00:49:27.340 align:middle line:84%
So the idea of this,
in the next slide,

00:49:27.340 --> 00:49:30.280 align:middle line:84%
is going to be to solve
for the plasmid number.

00:49:30.280 --> 00:49:34.750 align:middle line:84%
So slide 34, we have how you
would implement those two

00:49:34.750 --> 00:49:38.770 align:middle line:84%
equations that we had
in the last two slides--

00:49:38.770 --> 00:49:42.430 align:middle line:84%
are shown on the very top
left part of the slide.

00:49:42.430 --> 00:49:45.100 align:middle line:90%


00:49:45.100 --> 00:49:50.320 align:middle line:84%
dr dt is abbreviated dr
here in mathematical format.

00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:52.990 align:middle line:84%
It's that same k1
constant times n

00:49:52.990 --> 00:49:55.800 align:middle line:84%
is the concentration
of plasmid molecules.

00:49:55.800 --> 00:50:00.370 align:middle line:84%
Then the negative is
the degradation rate.

00:50:00.370 --> 00:50:04.600 align:middle line:84%
And the dilution by
cell division, mu.

00:50:04.600 --> 00:50:07.780 align:middle line:84%
And that's times r, which is the
concentration of the inhibitory

00:50:07.780 --> 00:50:09.640 align:middle line:90%
RNA 1.

00:50:09.640 --> 00:50:12.080 align:middle line:84%
And in an
[? analysis ?] equation,

00:50:12.080 --> 00:50:15.673 align:middle line:84%
which we've already seen before
for the plasmid molecules, n,

00:50:15.673 --> 00:50:17.965 align:middle line:84%
the change in concentration
of n is a function of time,

00:50:17.965 --> 00:50:23.130 align:middle line:84%
dn dt, here abbreviated
dn, is there.

00:50:23.130 --> 00:50:28.630 align:middle line:84%
And then we're
going to solve it.

00:50:28.630 --> 00:50:31.740 align:middle line:84%
So these first three things
are setting it up and asking

00:50:31.740 --> 00:50:34.320 align:middle line:90%
the program to solve it.

00:50:34.320 --> 00:50:35.910 align:middle line:84%
And we'll do it
under the constraints

00:50:35.910 --> 00:50:38.880 align:middle line:84%
of dr dt is equal to
0, dn dt is equal to 0.

00:50:38.880 --> 00:50:41.370 align:middle line:84%
You will recognize this as
the steady-state assumption.

00:50:41.370 --> 00:50:45.300 align:middle line:84%
Even though there are fluxes
in and out that are non-zero,

00:50:45.300 --> 00:50:47.970 align:middle line:90%
the net effect is zero.

00:50:47.970 --> 00:50:50.260 align:middle line:84%
And so that's the
formula for steady state.

00:50:50.260 --> 00:50:53.850 align:middle line:84%
We're going to start at
a dilution rate of 1,

00:50:53.850 --> 00:50:55.770 align:middle line:84%
where you'll have some
steady-state level.

00:50:55.770 --> 00:50:57.770 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to
watch the dynamics as it

00:50:57.770 --> 00:51:01.267 align:middle line:84%
goes to a dilution rate
that's slower, that is to say,

00:51:01.267 --> 00:51:02.350 align:middle line:90%
the growth rate is slower.

00:51:02.350 --> 00:51:04.500 align:middle line:84%
And as the growth
rate is slower,

00:51:04.500 --> 00:51:06.060 align:middle line:84%
you'll expect,
maybe, to accumulate

00:51:06.060 --> 00:51:07.140 align:middle line:90%
more plasmid molecules.

00:51:07.140 --> 00:51:10.230 align:middle line:84%
Because they're not in lockstep
with the cell division rate,

00:51:10.230 --> 00:51:12.360 align:middle line:90%
the cell grows more slowly.

00:51:12.360 --> 00:51:13.860 align:middle line:84%
And there's not
some other feedback,

00:51:13.860 --> 00:51:16.027 align:middle line:84%
and we haven't put any other
feedback in this model,

00:51:16.027 --> 00:51:18.380 align:middle line:84%
then it should go to
about twice the level.

00:51:18.380 --> 00:51:22.140 align:middle line:90%


00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:29.580 align:middle line:84%
So you do the symbolic
solver in the top here.

00:51:29.580 --> 00:51:33.280 align:middle line:84%
And you get this
symbolic solution here.

00:51:33.280 --> 00:51:34.740 align:middle line:84%
And if you do the
numeric solver,

00:51:34.740 --> 00:51:41.233 align:middle line:84%
NDSolve, then you get a
very similar solution.

00:51:41.233 --> 00:51:42.150 align:middle line:90%
And you could plot it.

00:51:42.150 --> 00:51:44.520 align:middle line:84%
Here you're plotting y,
which is a plasmid copy

00:51:44.520 --> 00:51:49.890 align:middle line:84%
number from over a
time range of 0 to 3.

00:51:49.890 --> 00:51:52.050 align:middle line:84%
And you can see it goes
up from slightly over 1

00:51:52.050 --> 00:51:57.000 align:middle line:84%
to slightly over 2 in terms
of a concentration of plasmid,

00:51:57.000 --> 00:52:04.140 align:middle line:84%
as you might expect from
lowering the dilution curve.

00:52:04.140 --> 00:52:08.100 align:middle line:84%
Just as we had stochastic
models for the bistability

00:52:08.100 --> 00:52:09.840 align:middle line:90%
that we talked about earlier--

00:52:09.840 --> 00:52:12.840 align:middle line:84%
the Xenopus bistability
could be continuous.

00:52:12.840 --> 00:52:17.040 align:middle line:84%
And the lambda model
could be stochastic.

00:52:17.040 --> 00:52:20.755 align:middle line:84%
Here there are stochastic models
for Copy Number Control, CNC,

00:52:20.755 --> 00:52:21.880 align:middle line:90%
which are very interesting.

00:52:21.880 --> 00:52:23.580 align:middle line:90%
I urge you to look at them--

00:52:23.580 --> 00:52:28.680 align:middle line:84%
where you can have, basically,
using stochastic modeling to

00:52:28.680 --> 00:52:31.410 align:middle line:84%
do molecular clocks,
where you can reduce

00:52:31.410 --> 00:52:32.880 align:middle line:90%
the rate at plasmid loss.

00:52:32.880 --> 00:52:35.850 align:middle line:84%
You can see, in that last one,
if you had a very small number

00:52:35.850 --> 00:52:41.340 align:middle line:84%
of molecules, you would have
a loss in some of the cells

00:52:41.340 --> 00:52:46.080 align:middle line:84%
that would be more accurately
modeled in a stochastic model.

00:52:46.080 --> 00:52:52.110 align:middle line:84%
Now, we want to go from these
models of red blood cell where

00:52:52.110 --> 00:52:54.630 align:middle line:84%
you have metabolism
without polymer synthesis,

00:52:54.630 --> 00:52:57.990 align:middle line:84%
and the CNC model where you
have polymer synthesis of RNA

00:52:57.990 --> 00:53:03.360 align:middle line:84%
and DNA, but without metabolism,
to a more integrated cell

00:53:03.360 --> 00:53:05.460 align:middle line:90%
where you have both going on.

00:53:05.460 --> 00:53:09.000 align:middle line:84%
And you want to represent
the full optimization that

00:53:09.000 --> 00:53:13.740 align:middle line:84%
must occur, getting metabolism
optimally suited so that you

00:53:13.740 --> 00:53:18.000 align:middle line:84%
get the right kinds
of macromolecules made

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:20.020 align:middle line:84%
in a complicated
cell like E. coli,

00:53:20.020 --> 00:53:23.010 align:middle line:84%
which can adjust to a wide
variety of different growth

00:53:23.010 --> 00:53:23.980 align:middle line:90%
conditions.

00:53:23.980 --> 00:53:25.650 align:middle line:90%
So what are the problems here?

00:53:25.650 --> 00:53:28.740 align:middle line:84%
The number of parameters
that we needed

00:53:28.740 --> 00:53:31.930 align:middle line:84%
and had for the red
blood cell was enormous.

00:53:31.930 --> 00:53:32.610 align:middle line:90%
It was 200.

00:53:32.610 --> 00:53:34.590 align:middle line:84%
It was a tour de
force to get those.

00:53:34.590 --> 00:53:38.820 align:middle line:84%
For E. coli, it's orders of
magnitude more that are needed.

00:53:38.820 --> 00:53:41.520 align:middle line:84%
Because instead
of 40 enzymes, we

00:53:41.520 --> 00:53:48.150 align:middle line:84%
have somewhere
between 400 and 4,000.

00:53:48.150 --> 00:53:49.920 align:middle line:84%
So measuring parameters
is a problem.

00:53:49.920 --> 00:53:53.040 align:middle line:84%
And we have the same problem
about in vitro versus in vivo.

00:53:53.040 --> 00:53:56.790 align:middle line:84%
We have the same
set of constraints.

00:53:56.790 --> 00:53:59.280 align:middle line:84%
And we want to focus
more of our attention

00:53:59.280 --> 00:54:05.080 align:middle line:90%
now on the flux constraints.

00:54:05.080 --> 00:54:07.950 align:middle line:84%
So after a short
break, we're going

00:54:07.950 --> 00:54:10.830 align:middle line:84%
to come back and talk about
the flux balance as a solution

00:54:10.830 --> 00:54:11.730 align:middle line:90%
to this.

00:54:11.730 --> 00:54:16.050 align:middle line:84%
And just as we had with
the red blood cell,

00:54:16.050 --> 00:54:18.000 align:middle line:84%
we're going to be
focusing in on ways

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:23.010 align:middle line:84%
to relook at the way we
think about the synthesis

00:54:23.010 --> 00:54:26.580 align:middle line:84%
and degradation of the
molecules in this network

00:54:26.580 --> 00:54:28.320 align:middle line:84%
to see if we can
rephrase in a way

00:54:28.320 --> 00:54:30.240 align:middle line:84%
where we can ask
interesting questions

00:54:30.240 --> 00:54:34.060 align:middle line:84%
about the optimization
of these systems.

00:54:34.060 --> 00:54:35.850 align:middle line:90%
So take a quick break.

00:54:35.850 --> 00:54:40.610 align:middle line:84%
And the second half, we'll
talk about the flux balance.

00:54:40.610 --> 00:54:43.000 align:middle line:90%